An operator $\opA\colon \reals^d \rightrightarrows \reals^d$ is \emph{monotone} if
\begin{align*}
    \langle \opA z - \opA z', z - z' \rangle \ge 0, \qquad \forall \, z,z'\in\reals^d ,
\end{align*}
where the notation means that $\langle u - v, z - z' \rangle \ge 0$ for any $u \in \opA z$ and $v \in \opA z'$.

For $\mu > 0$, an operator $\opA$ is $\mu$-\emph{strongly monotone} if
\begin{align*}
    \langle \opA z - \opA z', z - z' \rangle \ge \mu \|z-z'\|^2, \qquad \forall \, z,z'\in\reals^d .
\end{align*}

\begin{align}
